Pearson correlations between 5 variables in 2024, on the 343 observations with data for all of them.

The strongest positive correlation is between **Multidimensional poverty index (0-1)** and **Unsatisfied basic needs (% of population)** (r = 0.82): a strong association, in which units with more of one tend to have more of the other.

The strongest negative correlation is between **No poverty index (0-100)** and **Unsatisfied basic needs (% of population)** (r = -0.94): a strong association, in which units with more of one tend to have less of the other.

The weakest correlation, in either direction, is between **Energy poverty (% of dwellings)** and **Access to the 3 basic services (% of households)** (r = -0.46).

A coefficient near +1 or -1 means two variables move together closely, in the same or in opposite directions; near 0, that knowing one says little about the other. The table (`.gt`) sets Pearson correlations (above the diagonal) against Spearman rank correlations (below): a large gap between the two points to a non-linear relation or to outliers.

_These are associations, not causal effects. A causal reading needs a research design._
:::

